e but actually  counterproductive so what's the solution  how can we encourage innovation in a way  that you know actually works there are  two ways to look at patents first as a  right creating things takes time and  effort if you paint a picture or write a  story it belongs to you so why should  inventions be any different physically  or otherwise no one wants to be stolen  from this is why we use the term  intellectual property it's no different  than a house or a car which I'd argue is  kind of true a patent can be bought sold  licensed or abandoned but if ideas are  simply property the inventor of a  life-saving drug could just decide not  to share it and if companies deserve  monopolies over their creations or stuck  with the system we have sure in theory  we could adjust it shorten the length of  patents from twenty to ten or five or  even two years and patent trolls or be  more selective about which patents are  approved all of these would be a huge  help  but they wouldn't solve the fundamental  problem to do that we need to view them  not as a right but a trade you and I  promise to give companies a temporary  monopoly and they invest the time and  money to make the products we want but  were being ripped off were often giving  a monopoly when a company would have  made their product anyway in other words  or trading a lot for really nothing in  return would Apple stopped making  iPhones if they couldn't file patents  they might make less money but they'd  almost certainly still be profitable  even with copycats they could stand out  with things like better engineering  talent and marketing how do we know this  because it's already true Xiaomi makes  every attempt to replicate products like  the iPhone but I'd say Apple is still  doing ok or look at Lego whose patents  expired in 2011 but still makes billions  of dollars if anything losing them made  the company more in  of ativ by giving it better competition  of course this isn't always true a  pharmaceu